# TEG on a BITRUNK datapath tree — the "bbox-only is not a tap" vehicle
# (teg_multirect_status.md Final-state limitation 4, RESOLVED 2026-08-27).
#
# The legacy BITRUNK_H tree (two H rungs + a V backbone) over four endpoint
# blocks, one of which — r2 — is a `teg_mode over` multi-rect block whose two
# rects sit side by side ALONG the rung axis, sharing the rung's perp band.
#
# Until 2026-08-27 both BITRUNK families worked entirely on `orig_bbox`: no
# rect SELECTION and no TEG connection metal.  Both halves cost something real
# on this geometry, and they cost it in opposite directions:
#
#   • SELECTION: the "tap" was the UNION bbox face at the pin's along
#     coordinate — x=750, which on this block is the GAP between the rects.  A
#     tap on no metal is a silent open, and the generation coverage gate said
#     so by dropping the whole candidate (`[TopoGen] dropped 1 candidate(s)
#     … first open: BITRUNK_H missing block 'r2'`) whenever the union face fell
#     outside every rect — so a multi-rect design silently lost the datapath
#     shape it asked for.
#   • CONNECTION METAL: where the candidate did survive (this flow), rect#1 at
#     x 900..1000 lay beyond the rungs' along-span and NO placed metal of the
#     bundle touched it — TEG_OPEN at both placed stages (1 bundle-level at
#     nuts, 4 per-bit at dnuts).  That loudness is what made the scoping
#     acceptable; it is no longer needed.
#
# Both are now the TRUNK generator's own rules, read through the shared
# predicates: `best_rect` picks the tap rect, a rect sharing the spine's perp
# band is reached by GROWING the spine over its along-centre (a CROSSING, which
# needs no busterm seed — BITRUNK deliberately never calls annotate_endpoints,
# so it has no face anchor to fall back on), and every other rect gets one
# perpendicular stub from its facing face.  Here both rects share the y=50
# rung's band, so the rung itself is the connection metal: its span grows from
# x 750 to x 950 (rect#1's along-centre) and crosses both.
#
# The vehicle also guards the NUTS half.  `tighten_spans_to_reach` elects
# exactly ONE pass-through anchor per (bundle, BLOCK) — anchoring every
# crossing keeps phantom span for nothing (b44) — which rests on a block's
# rects being interchangeable covers, precisely what `teg_mode over` REVOKES.
# Without the per-rect anchor the generated rung reaches x=950 and the PLACED
# span comes back clipped to x=600 (rect#0's far face, the one elected
# crossing), so TEG_OPEN fires on placed geometry while the candidate is
# correct.  A regression in either half shows up here as a violation.
#
# Candidate pin: by TYPE (`BITRUNK_H`), the selector that survives a re-sorted
# pool — and this change DOES re-sort it, since the rung grew.
# test_flow_scripts.py::test_teg_bitrunk_flow_routes_clean asserts the planner
# line names it AND both audits report Success.

add_block src 0 0 100 100
add_block r1 300 300 400 400
add_block r2 rect 500 0 600 100 rect 900 0 1000 100 teg_mode over
add_block r3 300 600 400 700

def_layer 4 M4 H TOP 0
def_layer 5 M5 V TOP 0

def_track_pattern 4 0 (SIGNAL 2 2)x8
def_track_pattern 5 0 (SIGNAL 2 2)x8

add_bus d[4] src.tx r1.a,r2.b,r3.c

run_bundler STRICT
generate_topologies

# The legacy datapath tree: rungs at y=50 and y=650, backbone at x=300.  The
# y=50 rung spans x 50..950, crossing BOTH of r2's rects.
select_topology 1 BITRUNK_H

run_planner
run_nuts
check_design
run_detailed_nuts
check_design
report_wl
visualize
